1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:00,959
qwenagent.

2
00:00:00,959 --> 00:00:05,263
Can subagent like modules live inside a single forward pass of a frozen Qwen?

3
00:00:05,263 --> 00:00:09,399
This is a short tour of the idea, the task, what worked, and what did not.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:15,105
Agent frameworks get subagents by calling a model over and over from the outside.

5
00:00:15,105 --> 00:00:17,777
Here the workers live inside one forward pass.

6
00:00:17,777 --> 00:00:23,296
Each owns a private recurrent state, a learned router decides which worker sees each token, and

7
00:00:23,296 --> 00:00:25,503
no prompts or tool calls are involved.

8
00:00:25,503 --> 00:00:30,092
The question is whether routing to private slots actually helps state tracking.

9
00:00:31,090 --> 00:00:35,913
At layer twelve of a frozen Qwen three, the residual stream feeds a router and K slot states

10
00:00:35,913 --> 00:00:36,805
each a small GRU.

11
00:00:36,805 --> 00:00:41,576
A return network decodes the slots and adds the result back into the residual stream before

12
00:00:41,576 --> 00:00:43,254
layers thirteen to twenty seven.

13
00:00:43,254 --> 00:00:44,774
Only the workers are trained.

14
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:48,194
The task is interleaved register programs.

15
00:00:48,194 --> 00:00:53,678
Each register has its own stream of arithmetic operations, all shuffled together, and the model

16
00:00:53,678 --> 00:00:55,929
must report one register's final value.

17
00:00:55,929 --> 00:00:57,141
Chance is 10 percent.

18
00:00:57,141 --> 00:01:00,489
We train on two or three registers and test up to sixteen.

19
00:01:01,490 --> 00:01:03,776
Frozen Qwen alone sits at chance.

20
00:01:03,776 --> 00:01:09,871
With workers attached, accuracy at two registers is about 61.5 percent for the stateless

21
00:01:09,871 --> 00:01:14,789
adapter, 58.8 for a single shared state, and 55.4 for the routed slots.

22
00:01:14,789 --> 00:01:19,430
By sixteen registers they all fall to roughly 25.6 to 28.4 percent.

23
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:22,755
Five seeds per cell, with random register names.

24
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,914
Averaged over two to sixteen registers, the stateless adapter reaches 45.8 percent, the routed

25
00:01:29,914 --> 00:01:32,925
slots 41.0, and a parameter matched LoRA 39.4.

26
00:01:32,925 --> 00:01:36,329
The private slots do not beat the simpler baselines.

27
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:39,766
The workers are doing real work, though.

28
00:01:39,766 --> 00:01:45,187
If the return channel is zeroed at test time, every mode drops back to about 12.9 percent

29
00:01:45,187 --> 00:01:46,162
which is chance.

30
00:01:46,162 --> 00:01:48,964
The answer flows through the injected modules.

31
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:55,586
The learned router really does bind registers to slots, with about 88 percent purity on

32
00:01:55,586 --> 00:01:58,367
average, but one seed sits near fifty four.

33
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:01,600
Clean routing does not turn into an accuracy edge.

34
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,051
Scale helps a lot.

35
00:02:04,051 --> 00:02:11,307
At sixteen registers on Qwen three four B, the adapter reaches 56.3 percent and the single

36
00:02:11,307 --> 00:02:15,741
state 46.5, while the routed slots fall behind at 30.2.

37
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:17,191
Caveats.

38
00:02:17,191 --> 00:02:20,688
One task family at toy scale, with two to five seeds per cell.

39
00:02:20,688 --> 00:02:22,605
No best tuned LoRA claim was made.

40
00:02:22,605 --> 00:02:26,383
Every mode collapses when each register gets long operation chains.

41
00:02:26,383 --> 00:02:30,556
And the main claim, that private slots help, is not supported by the data.

42
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:34,552
Code, results and logs are in the repo.

